    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This past year, I joined participants of spiritual retreats in India and Spain. I was surprised to discover that\r\nin each location individuals who had undergone some sort of spiritual process (a process through which one\r\ndiscovers a sense of self or \"spirit\") reported similar emotional and behavior changes from the experience –\r\nparticularly individuals who walked the Camino de Santiago, a Catholic pilgrimage in Northern Spain, and\r\nthose who completed intensive, silent meditation retreats in India. My thesis will explore similarities and\r\ndifferences between the Art of Silence meditation retreat and the Camino de Santiago and participants'\r\nemotional outcomes. I will use both qualitative and quantitative data to document how practitioners changed\r\nduring the processes and identify what community dynamics, interactions, and individual behaviors contributed\r\nto their developmental process. I hope to answer my research question of how spiritual processes influence\r\nemotion and behavior.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Winfield, T., Rosenfeld, M., and Stanford University Sociology Department (2024). The Seekers: Do Spiritual Practices Lead to Higher Subjective Happiness?. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/vz275qb0935.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
